The misalignment gap. The space between what’s sent and what’s read; between a driver’s trajectory and the flow everyone expects; between the system’s current state and the one you’re trying to evoke.

That gap creates pressure—and pressure creates motion. Make it legible, and it can become a catalyst: the network notices the mismatch and reorganizes around it. Too little gap, no movement; too much, breakdown. The craft is tuning the mismatch so it produces a useful response.